{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Angeline - Slocan Ramblers
I am not aware of any other versions out or any of their songs posted out there, which is a shame.
hearing the song. I first heard these young Canadiens at the mother of all folk festivals, Merlefest,
in April 2016 and totally fell for them and their CD, Coffee Creek, released in 2015.
They are incredible musicians and I am sure we will be hearing more from them.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[D]Had a lot of faith when [G]I was [D]young
[D]Things were different where [G]I come [A]from
Never thought [G]much of [D]home, [G]Angeline, that [A]haunts me [D]now
[D]I left home      just to  [G]get    [D]away
[D]Find some work  and [G]earn some [A]pay
But [G]o-oh, God, the [D]years, [G]Angeline, I [A]lost my [D]way
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[D]There’s my [G]mother in her [D]letters. There’s my [G]father singin’  [D]tunes
[D]There’s my [G]Sisters and my [D]Brothers.  [G]Angeline,   [A]I think of [D]you
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[D]It ain’t hard to [G]find a [D]friend
[D]With cocaine and [G]money to [A]spend
[G]Drinkin’ and playing [D]cards, [G]Angeline,  [A]it fell [D]apart
[D]You can lose your [G]head so [D]fast,
[D]Crash like a freight train [G]comin’ off the [A]tracks
I [G]ki-illed a man that [D]night, [G]Angeline,    [A]I took his [D]life…
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[D]There’s my [G]mother in her [D]letters. There’s my [G]father singin’  [D]tunes
[D]There’s my [G]Sisters and my [D]Brothers.  [G]Angeline,   [A]I think of [D]you
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[D]Ain’t it funny how [G]things can [D]change
[D]And I wonder if [G]you feel the [A]same
Twenty long [G]ye-ars [D]remain, [G]Angeline,   [A]comin’ home to [D]you…
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[D]Home to my [G]mother, and her [D]letters. Home to [G]father, singin' [D]tunes
